# Known-run SDK namespace cessation

The existing Bug-search terminal endpoint accepts optional
`runtime_observation.observation_source`. Omission preserves the historical
native observation wire and canonical identity. The only new source is the
closed `sdk_namespace_exit` carrier (`KnownRunNamespaceExit`).

It retains an already accepted real session/run/Hermes tuple. Its terminal
status may be `stopped` or `cancelled` only: the source says the original
namespace ceased, and does not claim a Hermes terminal GET or business success.
The source includes the original context/generation, initial and stop operation
IDs, raw command hash, exact protective journal reference, registration hash,
original Base4 Running receipt and actual Base4 namespace-exit proof. Source
size is at most 16 KiB, proof size at most 8 KiB. Closed receipt/snapshot/engine
shapes reject arbitrary environment/process fields; Debug omits raw proofs.

SDK snapshot/proof digests use Base sorted compact ASCII JSON, including DEL
and surrogate escaping. Workflow snapshot/result identities continue to use
their existing UTF-8 canonical contract. The same synthetic Unicode-engine
fixture is copied into both independent packages. It is schema/hash evidence,
not evidence that any container was started or stopped.

The exact configured Fleet machine, immutable binding/fence and actual admitted
Workflow prepared receipt remain mandatory. No fresh human authorization is
required for protective closure after revocation. Unknown terminal POST reads
the original operation; original-key replay retains the same source and proof.
A later owner result attaches to the same observation and leaves the original
terminal receipt unchanged. No100/101 cancellation is used after a known run.

Fleet checks cancellation/revocation/deadline before native HTTP and records
`protective-context-stop` in the existing command journal. After its durable
claim, original SDK stop/reconciliation is independent of Hermes availability.
Workflow fencing is independently journaled and bounded best effort. Positive
SDK exit initially writes only a held command receipt. Capacity is released
only in the existing transaction accepting an actual Tracker terminal ACK.

Normal native completion and protective SDK cessation arbitrate the same
immutable `tracker-terminal` command under the original custody locks.
New private request candidates are addressed by the raw body hash; publication
and readback verify that hash. The first retained request wins; a racing loser
delivers the original winner instead of posting another body/key. Historical
fixed request files remain readable. Losing candidate files can occur only
during a genuine concurrent race, not on every retry.

An already retained native Completed/Failed/Cancelled observation is preserved
when an SDK-source release wins. A nonterminal run may become Cancelled after
the accepted cessation. Runtime cessation, actual native observation, coverage,
owner verdict and finding delivery stay separate.

New domain/app/owner-PG/Fleet storage tests are source only until the immutable
integration gate. No schema migration, endpoint, queue, deployment or runtime
capability is enabled by this slice.

